e, Nature 272 (1978) 35 [ INSPIRE ]., DOI: 10.1038/272035a0"}],"curated_relation":false,"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/139218"}}],"number_of_pages":54,"legacy_creation_date":"2018-08-10","preprint_date":"2018-08-08","author_count":3,"public_notes":[{"source":"arXiv","value":"54 pages, 10 figures. v2: a few comments improved and reference [52]\n  added. Matches published version"}],"earliest_date":"2018-08-08","refereed":true,"external_system_identifiers":[{"schema":"ADS","value":"2018JHEP...10..001D"},{"schema":"MSNET","value":"3892262"}],"facet_author_name":["992144_Harvey S. Reall","1021407_Oscar J.C. Dias","1060716_Jorge E. Santos"],"core":true,"license":[{"license":"arXiv nonexclusive-distrib 1.0","material":"preprint","url":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/"},{"license":"CC-BY-4.0","imposing":"Springer"}],"_oai":{"sets":["Literature"],"id":"oai:inspirehep.net:1685783","updated":"2023-10-03T08:50:23.607460"},"journal_title_variants":["JHEP"],"arxiv_eprints":[{"categories":["gr-qc","hep-th"],"value":"1808.02895"}],"referenced_authors_bais":["I.G.Moss.1","J.Natario.1","S.R.Dolan.1","A.Franzen.1","R.Monteiro.1","C.M.Chambers.1","Luca.Bombelli.1","W.Israel.1","G.S.Hartnett.1","S.J.Oh.1","S.A.Teukolsky.1","A.S.Miranda.1","Anil.Zenginoglu.1","R.C.Myers.1","W.H.Press.1","Y.Shlapentokh.Rothman.1","A.Vasy.1","F.Mellor.1","D.A.Nichols.1","P.M.Girao.1","Stephen.W.Hawking.1","L.Lehner.1","S.Hod.1","Yanbei.Chen.1","Kyriakos.Destounis.1","V.T.Zanchin.1","Paul.C.W.Davies.1","J.Lopes.Costa.1","P.R.Brady.1","Z.Zhang.219","B.Mashhoon.1","A.B.Zimmerman.1","R.Penrose.1","A.Ishibashi.1","Janna.J.Levin.1","G.W.Gibbons.1","K.M.Simpson.1","H.S.Reall.1","A.Ori.2","C.Kehle.1","E.Calzetta.1","O.J.C.Dias.1","V.Cardoso.1","Huan.Yang.1","Fan.Zhang.3","Larry.J.Romans.1","V.Ferrari.1","A.Jansen.2","Neil.J.Cornish.1","M.Dafermos.1","P.Figueras.1","E.Poisson.1","P.Hintz.1","J.E.Santos.1","Helvi.Witek.1","M.Giesler.1","J.Luk.1","Hideo.Kodama.1","F.Eperon.1","J.D.Silva.1","N.D.Birrell.1","Emanuele.Berti.1","B.Way.1"],"legacy_version":"20210504075357.0","inspire_categories":[{"term":"Gravitation and Cosmology","source":"arxiv"},{"term":"Gravitation and Cosmology"}],"first_author":{"emails":["ojcd1r13@soton.ac.uk"],"affiliations_identifiers":[{"schema":"GRID","value":"grid.5491.9"},{"schema":"ROR","value":"https://ror.org/01ryk1543"}],"full_name":"Dias, Oscar J.C.","ids":[{"schema":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-4855-4750"}],"last_name":"Dias","first_name":"Oscar J.C.","recid":1021407},"control_number":1685783,"dois":[{"source":"bibmatch","value":"10.1007/JHEP10(2018)001"},{"material":"publication","source":"arXiv","value":"10.1007/JHEP10(2018)001"}],"document_type":["article"],"texkeys":["Dias:2018etb"],"abstracts":[{"source":"Springer","value":"It has been argued that the strong cosmic censorship conjecture is violated by Reissner-Nordström-de Sitter black holes: for near-extremal black holes, generic scalar field perturbations arising from smooth initial data have finite energy at the Cauchy horizon even though they are not continuously differentiable there. In this paper, we consider the analogous problem for coupled gravitational and electromagnetic perturbations. We find that such perturbations exhibit a much worse violation of strong cosmic censorship: for a sufficiently large near-extremal black hole, perturbations arising from smooth initial data can be extended through the Cauchy horizon in an arbitrarily smooth way. This is in apparent contradiction with an old argument in favour of strong cosmic censorship. We resolve this contradiction by showing that this old argument is valid only for initial data that is not smooth. This is in agreement with the recent proposal that, to recover strong cosmic censorship, one must allow rough initial data.","abstract_source_suggest":{"input":"Springer"}},{"source":"arXiv","value":"It has been argued that the strong cosmic censorship conjecture is violated by Reissner-Nordstr\\\"om-de Sitter black holes: for near-extremal black holes, generic scalar field perturbations arising from smooth initial data have finite energy at the Cauchy horizon even though they are not continuously differentiable there. In this paper, we consider the analogous problem for coupled gravitational and electromagnetic perturbations. We find that such perturbations exhibit a much worse violation of strong cosmic censorship: for a sufficiently large near-extremal black hole, perturbations arising from smooth initial data can be extended through the Cauchy horizon in an arbitrarily smooth way. This is in apparent contradiction with an old argument in favour of strong cosmic censorship. We resolve this contradiction by showing that this old argument is valid only for initial data that is not smooth. This is in agreement with the recent proposal that, to recover strong cosmic censorship, one must allow rough initial data.","abstract_source_suggest":{"input":"arXiv"}}],"primary_arxiv_category":["gr-qc"],"titles":[{"source":"arXiv","title":"Strong cosmic censorship: taking the rough with the smooth"}],"imprints":[{"date":"2018-10-01"}],"curated":true},"created":"2018-08-10T00:00:00+00:00","id":"1685783","updated":"2023-10-03T08:50:23.607460+00:00","links":{"bibtex":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/1685783?format=bibtex","latex-eu":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/1685783?format=latex-eu","latex-us":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/1685783?format=latex-us","json":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/1685783?format=json","json-expanded":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/1685783?format=json-expanded","cv":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/1685783?format=cv","citations":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/?q=refersto%3Arecid%3A1685783"}},{"metadata":{"citation_count":0,"citation_count_without_self_citations":0,"authors":[{"raw_affiliations":[{"value":"Central Astronomical Observatory at Pulkovo of the Russian Academy of Sciences, St.Peter