ao.Feng.Wang.1","A.M.Geller.1","D.S.Wang.2","T.Aoki.12","B.Doucot.1","A.Dunsworth.1","M.Ohzeki.1","C.Beny.1","Peter.van.Loock.1","L.Eldar.1","Z.Nussinov.2","P.Schindler.1","T.O.White.1","Z.Leghtas.1","A.Lubotzky.1","F.T.Chong.1","A.Paetznick.1","L.C.L.Hollenberg.1","R.Laflamme.1","C.Y.Lai.1","M.Horodecki.1","S.Bravyi.2","J.Alicea.1","Jian.Wei.Pan.1","M.Hennrich.1","S.D.Franceschi.2","R.Horodecki.1","R.Koenig.2","M.A.Martin.Delgado.1","J.Dengis.1","Chao.Yang.Lu.1","M.Mariantoni.1","H.Chalabi.1","J.L.Jiang.1","C.Jones.11","D.Sank.1","R.van.Handel.1","Liang.Sun.1","Y.Y.Chen.1","D.Markham.1","M.Hatridge.1","C.Chamon.1","J.A.Smolin.1","J.Ghosh.3","S.Tarucha.1","E.Jeffrey.1","A.C.Doherty.1","A.V.Aho.1","G.Smith.6","Beni.Yoshida.1","Y.L.Zhou.1","S.Shankar.1","M.J.Kastoryano.1","M.Chwalla.1","D.S.Pavlichin.1","Wei.Bo.Gao.1","R.Barends.1","L.Bouten.1","J.Preskill.1","X.C.Yao.1","J.Kerckhoff.1","John.M.Martinis.1","Gavin.K.Brennen.1","Zeng.Bing.Chen.1","K.M.Sliwa.1","L.P.Pryadko.1","W.K.Wootters.1","Klemens.Hammerer.1","J.M.Elzerman.1","T.Monz.1","G.Takahashi.1","D.Olaya.3","E.A.Martinez.2","O.Oreshkov.2","D.W.Nigg.1","David.Bacon.1","J.Koch.1","K.Fujii.1","B.Leemhuis.1","S.T.Flammia.1","K.P.Michnicki.1","C.Castelnovo.2","T.M.Yu.1","S.Burton.1","L.M.K.Vandersypen.1","J.M.Geremia.1","C.Wang.87","J.J.E.Slotine.1","Z.H.Chen.1","D.Loss.1","A.Blais.1","A.C.Whiteside.1","J.Kubiatowicz.1","M.Mueller.5","B.M.Terhal.3","B.Campbell.6","F.Solgun.1","S.B.Bravyi.1","H.K.Ng.1","B.Zeng.1","J.S.Greidanus.1","A.E.B.Nielsen.1","S.K.Goyal.1","F.P.Brooks.1","M.Mueller.2","A.G.Fowler.2","A.Fowler.1","L.H.W.van.Beveren.1","Xiao.Gang.Wen.1","G.Kirchmair.1","L.P.Kouwenhoven.1","F.Pastawski.1","C.H.Bennett.1","M.A.Levin.1","M.S.Bullock.1","D.W.Kribs.1","P.Roushan.1","S.Glancy.1","M.Herold.1","A.Narla.1","A.Houck.1","T.J.Anderson.1","David.Schuster.1","J.i.Yoshikawa.1","P.Zoller.1","N.Imoto.2","G.M.S.Vasconcelos.1","Akira.Furusawa.1","B.L.Ioffe.1","J.P.OMalley.1","J.Haah.1","S.E.Nigg.2","J.C.Napp.1","David.A.Meyer.1","K.Lalumiere.1","B.Chiaro.1","J.Harrington.4","Robert.Raussendorf.1","J.Y.Mutus.1","J.R.Wootton.1","R.Van.Meter.1","M.B.Hastings.2","H.Bombin.1","B.A.Chase.1","C.Neill.1","M.A.Nielsen.2","A.M.Stephens.2","N.C.Menicucci.1","P.Horodecki.1","D.W.Leung.1","M.Freedman.1","E.Knill.1","S.M.Girvin.1","You.Jin.Deng.1","W.H.Zurek.1","J.Majer.1","B.A.Bell.1","Michel.H.Devoret.1","D.Aharonov.1","Z.Chen.136","A.L.McInnes.1","A.Vainsencher.1","E.T.Campbell.1","S.Michalakis.1","W.J.Wadsworth.1","j.P.Tillich.1","J.Dommen.1","Y.Yamamoto.1","R.Hanson.1","M.S.Tame.1","F.Hassler.5","N.C.Jones.1","E.DupontFerrier.1","E.Dennis.1","M.Kitagawa.1","Andrew.M.Steane.1","A.Faruque.1","B.W.Reichardt.1","Alexey.Petrenko.1","M.Muller.12"],"figures":[{"filename":"fig2_baconshor33.png","material":"preprint","caption":"(Color Online) The 9-qubit $[[9,1,3]]$ Shor code with black qubits on the vertices. The stabilizer of Shor's code is generated by the weight-2 $Z$-checks as well as two weight-6, double row, $X$-checks ${\\bf X}_{=,1}=X_1 X_2 X_3 X_4 X_5 X_6$ and ${\\bf X}_{=,2}$. An alternative way of measuring ${\\bf X}_{=,1}$ and ${\\bf X}_{=,2}$ is by measuring the weight-2 $X$-checks in the Figure. One can similarly define two weight-6, double column, $Z$-checks, ${\\bf Z}_{||,1}$ and ${\\bf Z}_{||,2}$ as products of elementary weight-2 $Z$ checks. See also Fig.~\\ref{fig:BS}.","label":"fig:BS1","source":"arxiv","key":"907067482229bb065485f5b687e1ae5a","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/907067482229bb065485f5b687e1ae5a"},{"filename":"fig3_codewords_qubit.png","material":"preprint","caption":"Picture from \\cite{GKP}: Amplitude of codewords for the stabilizer code with commuting checks $S_q(\\alpha)=e^{2i \\pi \\hat{q}/\\alpha}$ and $S_p(\\alpha)=e^{-2i \\hat{p} \\alpha}$ which encodes a qubit in an oscillator.","label":"fig:osc","source":"arxiv","key":"310b08576f2cdcf4f39d4222f877797f","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/310b08576f2cdcf4f39d4222f877797f"},{"filename":"fig7_surface01.png","material":"preprint","caption":"(Color Online) Surface code on a $L \\times L$ lattice. On every edge of the (black) lattice there is a qubit, in total $L^2+(L-1)^2$ qubits (depicted is $L=8$). Two types of local parity checks, $A_s$ and $B_p$, each act on four qubits, except at the boundary where they act on three qubits. The subspace of states which satisfy the parity checks is two-dimensional and hence represents a qubit. $\\overline{Z}$ is any $Z$-string connecting the north to the south boundary, which is referred to as `rough', while $\\overline{X}$ is any $X$-string connecting the east to west `smooth' boundary running through vertices of the dual lattice.","label":"fig:sur","source":"arxiv","key":"5edad48c35d56feb1e0ee79dbdb436ad","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/5edad48c35d56feb1e0ee79dbdb436ad"},{"filename":"fig9_Hadamard.png","material":"preprint","caption":"Sketch of the Hadamard gate on a sheet which encodes a single logical qubit as in Fig.~\\ref{fig:sur}, see also \\cite{horsman+:suture}. After doing a Hadamard gate on each qubit, the rotated code lattice is gradually rotated back to its original orientation by adding and taking away qubits and stabilizer checks at the boundaries.","label":"fig:hadamard","source":"arxiv","key":"85f3fbfd71ccd7e26bab2ec0b60e975b","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/85f3fbfd71ccd7e26bab2ec0b60e975b"},{"filename":"fig13_surface_code_holes.png","material":"preprint","caption":"(Color Online) (a) A smooth hole is created by removing a block of plaquette operators and the star operators acting on qubits in the interior of the block. The $Z$-loop around the hole is $\\overline{Z}$ while $\\overline{X}$ is an $X$-string to the boundary. The qubits inside the hole (4 in the picture) are decoupled from the lattice. (b) Two smooth holes can make one smooth qubit and two rough holes can make one rough qubit so that moving a smooth hole around a rough hole realizes a CNOT gate.","label":"fig:surhole","source":"arxiv","key":"6ee63cbfa031537510447d3f02f74e3f","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/6ee63cbfa031537510447d3f02f74e3f"},{"filename":"fig14_baconshor55.png","material":"preprint","caption":"(Color Online) (a) $[[16,1,4]]$ Bacon-Shor code with $\\overline{X}$, a row of $X$s, and $\\overline{Z}$, a column of $Z$s. The stabilizer generators are double columns of $Z$s, ${\\bf Z}_{||,i}$ (one is depicted) and double rows of $X$s, ${\\bf X}_{=,j}$.(b) Decoding for $X$ errors (or $Z$ errors in the orthogonal direction). Black dots denote the places where the double column parity checks ${\\bf Z}_{||,i}$ have eigenvalue $-1$ (defects). The $X$ error string $E$ has $X$ errors in the fattened (red) region and no errors elsewhere and $E_c$ is its complement. Clearly the string $E$ has lower weight than $E_c$ and is chosen as the likely error.","label":"fig:BS","source":"arxiv","key":"4ff0fb1ea24463b8d8e8a8002ad14394","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/4ff0fb1ea24463b8d8e8a8002ad14394"},{"filename":"fig15_baconshor33ancilla.png","material":"preprint","caption":"(Color Online) (a) In order to measure the $XX$ and $ZZ$ operators one can place ancilla qubits (open dots) in between the data qubits. Such ancilla qubit interacts with the two adjacent data qubits to collect the syndrome. (b) Alternatively, to measure ${\\bf Z}_{||,i}$ one can prepare a 3-qubit entangled cat state $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(\\ket{000}+\\ket{111})$ ((green) vertical line of dots) which interacts locally with the adjacent system qubits. ${\\bf X}_{=,1}$ could be measured by preparing a cat state for the ancilla qubits placed at, say, the horizontal line of (red) dots. The ancilla qubits at the open dots can be used to prepare the cat states.","label":"fig:BSancilla","source":"arxiv","key":"b8b07c5794f5a84fa618cb9e5e6f5cde","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/b8b07c5794f5a84fa618cb9e5e6f5cde"},{"filename":"fig16_surface_code_osc02.png","material":"preprint","caption":"(Color Online) Small example of the oscillator surface code where oscillators on the edges are locally coupled with plaquette and star operators so as to define an encoded oscillator with logical, non-local, displacements $\\overline{X}(d)$ and $\\overline{Z}(c)$. The realization of the four-oscillator interaction will require strong 4-mode squeezing in either position (at plaquettes) or momenta (at stars).","label":"fig:surf_osc","source":"arxiv","key":"20d4640fb82e009a2d93200feb8c618c","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/20d4640fb82e009a2d93200feb8c618c"},{"filename":"fig17_surf_sub.png","material":"preprint","caption":"(Color Online) Picture from \\cite{BDPS:3qubitcheck}: subsystem surface code on a lattice of size $L \\times L$ with $L^2$ square plaquettes (depicted is $L=3$). The qubits live on the edges and vertices of the plaquettes and are acted upon by weight-3 $X$ and $Z$-triangle operators (which are modified to become weight-2 operators at the boundary). The stabilizer checks are weight 6 except at the boundary.","label":"fig:surfsub","source":"arxiv","key":"f4dc08de1944add1a791356024d3b301","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/f4dc08de1944add1a791356024d3b301"}],"inspire_categories":[{"term":"Quantum Physics","source":"arxiv"}],"first_author":{"full_name":"Terhal, Barbara M.","last_name":"Terhal","first_name":"Barbara M.","recid":2073083},"control_number":2728418,"dois":[{"source":"APS","value":"10.1103/RevModPhys.87.307"},{"material":"publication","source":"arXiv","value":"10.1103/RevModPhys.87.307"}],"document_type":["article"],"texkeys":["Terhal:2013vbm"],"abstracts":[{"source":"APS","value":"It may seem inevitable that highly entangled quantum states are susceptible to disturbance through interaction with a decohering environment. However, certain multiqubit entangled states are well protected from common forms of decoherence as the quantum information is hidden in inherently nonlocal degrees of freedom. This review shows that this robustness is enabled by specific measurements on subsets of qubits, implementing a quantum version of an error correction process. Beginning with the basics, the latest understanding of the relation between this form of error correction and the concept of two-dimensional topological order in many-body physics is reviewed.","abstract_source_suggest":{"input":"APS"}},{"source":"arXiv","value":"Active quantum error correction using qubit stabilizer codes has emerged as a promising, but experimentally challenging, engineering program for building a universal quantum computer. In this review we consider the formalism of qubit stabilizer and subsystem stabilizer codes and their possible use in protecting quantum information in a quantum memory. We review the theory of fault-tolerance and quantum error-correction, discuss examples of various codes and code constructions, the general quantum error correction conditions, the noise threshold, the special role played by Clifford gates and the route towards fault-tolerant universal quantum computation. The second part of the review is focused on providing an overview of quantum error correction using two-dimensional (topological) codes, in particular the surface code architecture. We discuss the complexity of decoding and the notion of passive or self-correcting quantum memories. The review does not focus on a particular technology but discusses topics that will be relevant for various quantum technologies.","abstract_source_suggest":{"input":"arXiv"}}],"primary_arxiv_category":["quant-ph"],"titles":[{"source":"APS","title":"Quantum error correction for quantum memories"},{"source":"arXiv","title":"Quantum Error Correction for Quantum Memories"}],"imprints":[{"date":"2015-04-07"}],"curated":true},"created":"2023-11-30T17:13:22.196836+00:00","id":"2728418","updated":"2026-04-16T12:17:21.142741+00:00","links":{"bibtex":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/2728418?format=bibtex","latex-eu":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/2728418?format=latex-eu","latex-us":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/2728418?format=latex-us","json":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/2728418?format=json","json-expanded":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/2728418?format=json-expanded","cv":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/2728418?format=cv","citations":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/?q=refersto%3Arecid%3A2728418"}},{"metadata":{"citation_count":133,"citation_count_without_self_citations":99,"authors":[{"full_name_unicode_normalized":"socolowski, o., jr.","full_name":"Socolowski, O., Jr.","record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/authors/1056332"},"last_name":"Socolowski","affiliations":[{"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/institutions/903186"},"value":"Sao Paulo U."}],"ids":[{"schema":"INSPIRE BAI","value":"O.Socolowski.1"}],"signature_block":"SACALASCo","uuid":"a0388b97-e79d-4a49-8dd4-b4cea0aa8397","first_name":"O., Jr.","recid":1056332},{"full_name_unicode_normalized":"grassi, f.","full_name":"Grassi, F.","curated_relation":true,"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/authors/1007699"},"last_name":"Grassi","affiliations":[{"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/institutions/903186"},"value":"Sao Paulo U."}],"ids":[{"schema":"INSPIRE BAI","value":"F.Grassi.1"}],"signature_block":"GRASf","uuid":"75baf598-33fa-44ef-997b-73a942f57dc8","first_name":"F.","recid":1007699},{"full_name_unicode_normalized":"hama, yogiro","full_name":"Hama, Yogiro","curated_relation":true,"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/authors/1006889"},"last_name":"Hama","affiliations":[{"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/institutions/903186"},"value":"Sao Paulo U."}],"ids":[{"schema":"INSPIRE BAI","value":"Y.Hama.2"}],"signature_block":"HANy","uuid":"3df32183-44df-43a3-9148-f004a54d2e8d","first_name":"Yogiro","recid":1006889},{"full_name_unicode_normalized":"kodama, t.","full_name":"Kodama, T.","curated_relation":true,"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/authors/1002459"},"last_name":"Kodama","affiliations":[{"record":{"$ref":"https://i