, 052314 (2001)"]},"curated_relation":false,"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/499528"}},{"reference":{"label":"39","publication_info":{"journal_volume":"1509","artid":"341","year":1999,"page_start":"341","journal_title":"Science"},"misc":["W.O gburn,and \\Topologicalquantum com putation,\" Lecture N otes in Com puter"],"authors":[{"full_name":"J.Preskill"}]}},{"reference":{"arxiv_eprint":"quant-ph/0101025","label":"40","misc":["an,A.K itaev,M.J.Larsen,and Z.W ang, \\Topologicalquantum com putation,\" (2001)"],"authors":[{"full_name":"M.H.Freedm"}]},"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/2727221"}},{"reference":{"label":"41","misc":["\\Stable and attractive trajectories in m ulticom ponent system s,\" in Advances in Probability 6,ed. R.L.D obrushin  N ew York D ekke,1980),p.549-575"],"authors":[{"full_name":"A.L.Toom"}]}}],"number_of_pages":39,"legacy_creation_date":"2001-10-29","preprint_date":"2001-10","author_count":4,"public_notes":[{"source":"arXiv","value":"39 pages, 21 figures, REVTeX"}],"earliest_date":"2001-10","refereed":true,"external_system_identifiers":[{"schema":"MSNET","value":"1924451"},{"schema":"ADS","value":"2002JMP....43.4452D"},{"schema":"SPIRES","value":"SPIRES-4755090"}],"facet_author_name":["2240645_Eric Dennis","992791_John P. Preskill","1020823_Andrew J. Landahl","1056433_Alexi Y. Kitaev"],"core":true,"_oai":{"sets":["Literature"],"id":"oai:inspirehep.net:565014","updated":"2025-10-01T11:11:08.105751"},"journal_title_variants":["J. Math. Phys.","J.Math.Phys."],"arxiv_eprints":[{"categories":["quant-ph","cond-mat.stat-mech","hep-lat","hep-th"],"value":"quant-ph/0110143"}],"referenced_authors_bais":["John.March.Russell.1","David.A.Meyer.1","E.M.Rains.1","M.BenOr.1","M.Freedman.1","E.Knill.1","W.H.Zurek.1","E.Dennis.1","G.S.Agarwal.1","J.Preskill.1","Andrew.M.Steane.1","J.M.Larsen.1","D.Aharonov.1","D.Gottesman.1","A.R.Calderbank.1","H.Nishimori.1","P.W.Shor.1","N.J.A.Sloane.1","R.Impagliazzo.1","A.Y.Kitaev.1","Zheng.Han.Wang.1","K.M.Lee.2","R.Laflamme.1","N.Nisan.1","Mark.G.Alford.1"],"figures":[{"filename":"checks2.png","material":"preprint","caption":"Check operators of the toric code. Each plaquette operator is a tensor product of $Z$'s acting on the four links contained in the plaquette. Each site operator is a tensor product of $X$'s acting on the four links that meet at the site.","label":"fig_checks","source":"arxiv","key":"b82a51d304efc22ad3d7d4ddf3ff96ba","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/b82a51d304efc22ad3d7d4ddf3ff96ba"},{"filename":"homo2.png","material":"preprint","caption":"Cycles on the lattice. $(a)$ A homologically trivial cycle bounds a region that can be tiled by plaquettes. The corresponding tensor product of $Z$'s lies in the stabilizer of the toric code. $(b)$ A homologically nontrivial cycle is not a boundary. The corresponding tensor product of $Z$'s commutes with the stabilizer but is not contained in it. It is a logical operation that acts nontrivially in the code subspace.","label":"fig_homo","source":"arxiv","key":"88d3bfed78f6fccc09a950c65cc0ca9a","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/88d3bfed78f6fccc09a950c65cc0ca9a"},{"filename":"logical2.png","material":"preprint","caption":"Basis for the operators that act on the two encoded qubits of the toric code. The logical operators $\\bar Z_1$ and $\\bar Z_2$ are tensor products of $Z$'s associated with the fundamental nontrivial cycles of the torus constructed from links of the lattice. The complementary operators $\\bar X_1$ and $\\bar X_2$ are tensor products of $X$'s associated with  nontrivial cycles constructed from links of the dual lattice.","label":"fig_logical","source":"arxiv","key":"8acf3a6aec7e699c1c9abbc480f29d3e","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/8acf3a6aec7e699c1c9abbc480f29d3e"},{"filename":"defect2.png","material":"preprint","caption":"The highly ambiguous syndrome of the toric code. The two site defects shown could arise from errors on either one of the two chains shown. In general, error chains with the same boundary generate the same syndrome, and error chains that are homologically equivalent act on the code space in the same way.","label":"fig_defect","source":"arxiv","key":"5386c7f9541d4a08dd1f3262a1dd062c","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/5386c7f9541d4a08dd1f3262a1dd062c"},{"filename":"planar3.png","material":"preprint","caption":"A planar quantum code. $(a)$ At the top and bottom are the ``plaquette edges'' (or ``rough edges'') where there are three-qubit plaquette operators, and at the left and right are the ``site edges'' (or ``smooth edges'') where there are three-qubit site operators. The logical operation $\\bar Z$ for the one encoded qubit is a tensor product of $Z$'s acting on a chain running from one rough edge to the other, and the logical operation $\\bar X$ is a tensor product of $X$'s acting on a chain of the dual lattice running from one smooth edge to the other. For the lattice shown, the code's distance is $L=8$. $(b)$ Site and plaquette defects can appear singly, rather than in pairs. An isolated site defect arises from an error chain that ends at a rough edge, and an isolated plaquette defect arises from a dual error chain that ends at a smooth edge.","label":"fig_planar","source":"arxiv","key":"1f6be3daacd8d36e7a6e1da3495ee387","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/1f6be3daacd8d36e7a6e1da3495ee387"},{"filename":"dilute2.png","material":"preprint","caption":"Pairs of defects. If the error rate is small and errors on distinct links are uncorrelated, then connected error chains are typically short and the positions of defects are highly correlated. It is relatively easy to guess how the defects should be paired up so that each pair is the boundary of a connected chain.","label":"fig_dilute","source":"arxiv","key":"e03883d568420989e27d71d763d763a0","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/e03883d568420989e27d71d763d763a0"},{"filename":"ghosts2.png","material":"preprint","caption":"Ghost defects. Since faults can occur in the measurement of the error syndrome, the measured syndrome includes both genuine defects (lightly shaded) associated with actual errors and phony ``ghost defects'' (darkly shaded) that arise at randomly distributed locations. To perform recovery successfully, we need to be able to distinguish reliably between the genuine defects and the ghost defects. The position that is shaded both lightly and darkly represents a genuine defect that goes unseen due to a measurement error.","label":"fig_ghosts","source":"arxiv","key":"2e04101e079280a6c50ff20b0ec8bbb2","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/2e04101e079280a6c50ff20b0ec8bbb2"},{"filename":"syndrome_history.png","material":"preprint","caption":"The two-dimensional lattice depicting a history of the error syndrome for the quantum repetition code, with time running upward. Each row represents the syndrome at a particular time. Qubits reside on plaquettes, and two-qubit check operators are measured at each vertical link. Links where the syndrome is nontrivial are shaded.","label":"fig:syndrome_history","source":"arxiv","key":"75c4b957d03b58d8dee55cbd065fcf48","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/75c4b957d03b58d8dee55cbd065fcf48"},{"filename":"error_history.png","material":"preprint","caption":"An error history shown together with the syndrome history that it generates, for the quantum repetition code. Links where errors occured are darkly shaded, and links where the syndrome is nontrivial are lightly shaded. Errors on horizontal links indicate where a qubit flipped between successive syndrome measurements, and errors on vertical links indicate where the syndrome measurement was wrong. Vertical links that are shaded both lightly and darkly are locations where a nontrivial syndrome was found erroneously. The chain of lightly shaded links (the syndrome) and the chain of darkly shaded links (the errors) both have the same boundary","label":"fig:error_history","source":"arxiv","key":"b216791f461506f153383fd7e96072f8","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/b216791f461506f153383fd7e96072f8"},{"filename":"ising_error.png","material":"preprint","caption":"The ``quenched'' error chain $E$ and the ``fluctuating'' error chain $E'$, as represented in the two-dimensional random-bond Ising model. Ising spins taking values in $\\{\\pm 1\\}$ reside on plaquettes, Ising vortices are located on the sites marked by filled circles, and the coupling between neighboring spins is antiferromagnetic along the path $E$ that connects the Ising vortices. The links of $E'$ comprise a domain wall connecting the vortices. The closed path $C=E+E'$ encloses a domain of spins with the value $-1$.","label":"fig:ising_error","source":"arxiv","key":"ed23c06a34e16dc4d270dd8a875c5d84","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/ed23c06a34e16dc4d270dd8a875c5d84"},{"filename":"nishi8.png","material":"preprint","caption":"The phase diagram of the random-bond Ising model, with the temperature $\\beta^{-1}$ on the vertical axis and the probability $p$ of an antiferromagnetic bond on the horizontal axis. The solid line is the boundary between the ferromagnetic (ordered) phase and the paramagnetic (disordered) phase. The dotted line is the Nishimori line $e^{-2\\beta}= p/(1-p)$, which crosses the phase boundary at the Nishimori point $N$. From the point $N$ to the horizontal axis, the phase boundary is {\\em vertical}.","label":"fig:nishi","source":"arxiv","key":"138a9fa4af0265bcdc9bf0b153c26bca","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/138a9fa4af0265bcdc9bf0b153c26bca"},{"filename":"min_chain.png","material":"preprint","caption":"The error chain $E$ (darkly shaded) and one possible choice for the chain $E_{\\rm min}$ (lightly shaded), illustrated for a $6\\times 6$ torus in two dimensions. In this case $E+E_{\\rm min}$ contains a homologically nontrivial cycle of length 8, which contains $H_e=4$ links of $E$ and $H_m=4$ links of $E_{\\rm min}$.","label":"fig:min_chain","source":"arxiv","key":"76cab457d4b83d0015267648779d35fd","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/76cab457d4b83d0015267648779d35fd"},{"filename":"overlap.png","material":"preprint","caption":"The ``overlapping recovery'' method, shown schematically. All monopoles (boundary points of the error syndrome chain) are indicated as filled circles, including both monopoles left over from earlier rounds of error recovery (those in the shaded region below the dotted line) and monopoles generated after the previous round (those in the unshaded region above the dotted line). Also shown is the minimum weight chain $E'$ that connects each monopole to either another monopole or to the current time slice. The chain $E'$ contains $E'_{\\rm old}$, whose boundary lies entirely in the shaded region, and the remainder $E'_{\\rm keep}$. In the current recovery step, errors are corrected on the horizontal links of $E'_{\\rm old}$, and its boundary is then erased from the recorded syndrome history. The boundary of $E'_{\\rm keep}$ is retained in the record, to be dealt with in a future recovery step.","label":"fig:overlap","source":"arxiv","key":"9a2df747898d2557eb66c834468cc9b7","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/9a2df747898d2557eb66c834468cc9b7"},{"filename":"zip.png","material":"preprint","caption":"Building a distance-$(L+1)$ planar code by adding qubits to a distance-$L$ planar code. (Here, $L=5$.) In the first step, new two-qubit stabilizer operators are added in the corners with single CNOT's; in subsequent steps, three-qubit stabilizer operators are added with double CNOT's. The last step promotes the corner operators to three-qubit operators.","label":"fig:build_up","source":"arxiv","key":"09a8ebd405804a7f7ced1e04869511b8","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/09a8ebd405804a7f7ced1e04869511b8"},{"filename":"planar_hadamard.png","material":"preprint","caption":"Action of the bitwise Hadamard gate on the planar code. If Hadamard gates are applied simultaneously to all the qubits in the block, an ``NS code'' with rough edges at the north and south is transformed to an ``EW code'' with rough edges at the east and west; the encoded operation $\\bar Z_{\\rm NS}$ of the NS code is transformed to $\\bar X_{\\rm EW}$ of the EW code, and $\\bar X_{\\rm NS}$ is transformed to $\\bar Z_{\\rm EW}$.","label":"fig:hadamard","source":"arxiv","key":"473f2f35781f52732877f04b320b62eb","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/473f2f35781f52732877f04b320b62eb"},{"filename":"droplets.png","material":"preprint","caption":"Droplets of flipped qubits in the two-dimensional quantum repetition code. Qubits reside on plaquettes, and the qubits that have been flipped are shaded. Thick links are locations of ``defects'' where the error syndrome is nontrivial because neighboring qubits are anti-aligned. The defects form closed loops that enclose the droplets.","label":"fig_droplets","source":"arxiv","key":"d992cd62b29384949a7ab201feecba0c","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/d992cd62b29384949a7ab201feecba0c"}],"legacy_version":"20180914191009.0","inspire_categories":[{"term":"General Physics"},{"term":"Quantum Physics","source":"arxiv"}],"first_author":{"affiliations_identifiers":[{"schema":"ROR","value":"https://ror.org/00hx57361"}],"full_name":"Dennis, Eric","last_name":"Dennis","first_name":"Eric","recid":2240645},"control_number":565014,"dois":[{"value":"10.1063/1.1499754"},{"material":"publication","source":"arXiv","value":"10.1063/1.1499754"}],"document_type":["article"],"texkeys":["Dennis:2001nw"],"abstracts":[{"value":"We analyze surface codes, the topological quantum error-correcting codes introduced by Kitaev. In these codes, qubits are arranged in a two-dimensional array on a surface of nontrivial topology, and encoded quantum operations are associated with nontrivial homology cycles of the surface. We formulate protocols for error recovery, and study the efficacy of these protocols. An order-disorder phase transition occurs in this system at a nonzero critical value of the error rate: if the error rate is below the critical value (the accuracy threshold), encoded information can be protected arbitrarily well in the limit of a large code block. This phase transition can be accurately modeled by a three-dimensional Z_2 lattice gauge theory with quenched disorder. We estimate the accuracy threshold, assuming that all quantum gates are local, that qubits can be measured rapidly, and that polynomial-size classical computations can be executed instantaneously. We also devise a robust recovery procedure that does not require measurement or fast classical processing: however for this procedure the quantum gates are local only if the qubits are arranged in four or more spatial dimensions. We discuss procedures for encoding, measurement, and performing fault-tolerant universal quantum computation with surface codes, and argue that these codes provide a promising framework for quantum computing architectures."},{"source":"arXiv","value":"We analyze surface codes, the topological quantum error-correcting codes introduced by Kitaev. In these codes, qubits are arranged in a two-dimensional array on a surface of nontrivial topology, and encoded quantum operations are associated with nontrivial homology cycles of the surface. We formulate protocols for error recovery, and study the efficacy of these protocols. An order-disorder phase transition occurs in this system at a nonzero critical value of the error rate; if the error rate is below the critical value (the accuracy threshold), encoded information can be protected arbitrarily well in the limit of a large code block. This phase transition can be accurately modeled by a three-dimensional Z_2 lattice gauge theory with quenched disorder. We estimate the accuracy threshold, assuming that all quantum gates are local, that qubits can be measured rapidly, and that polynomial-size classical computations can be executed instantaneously. We also devise a robust recovery procedure that does not require measurement or fast classical processing; however for this procedure the quantum gates are local only if the qubits are arranged in four or more spatial dimensions. We discuss procedures for encoding, measurement, and performing fault-tolerant universal quantum computation with surface codes, and argue that these codes provide a promising framework for quantum computing architectures.","abstract_source_suggest":{"input":"arXiv"}}],"primary_arxiv_category":["quant-ph"],"titles":[{"title":"Topological quantum memory"},{"source":"arXiv","title":"Topological quantum memory"}],"curated":true},"created":"2001-10-29T00:00:00+00:00","id":"565014","updated":"2025-10-01T11:11:08.105751+00:00","links":{"bibtex":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/565014?format=bibtex","latex-eu":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/565014?format=latex-eu","latex-us":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/565014?format=latex-us","json":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/565014?format=json","json-expanded":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/565014?format=json-expanded","cv":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/565014?format=cv","citations":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/?q=refersto%3Arecid%3A565014"}},{"metadata":{"citation_count":26,"citation_count_without_self_citations":17,"authors":[{"raw_affiliations":[{"value":"Institute of Physics, Johannes Gutenberg-Universität Mainz, Staudingerweg 7, 55128 Mainz, Germany"}],"full_name_unicode_normalized":"schmidt, frank","full_name":"Schmidt, Frank","record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/authors/989835"},"affiliations":[{"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/institutions/911853"},"value":"Mainz U."}],"last_name":"Schmidt","ids":[{"schema":"INSPIRE BAI","value":"Frank.Schmidt.1"}],"first_name":"Frank","uuid":"14739b67-686f-42f6-8787-3326baad1b21","recid":989835},{"raw_affiliations":[{"value":"Institute of Physics, Johannes Gutenberg-Universität Mainz, Staudingerweg 7, 55128 Mainz, Germany"}],"full_name_unicode_normalized":"van loock, peter","full_name":"van Loock, Peter","record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/authors/2566049"},"affiliations":[{"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/institutions/911853"},"value":"Mainz U."}],"last_name":"van Loock","ids":[{"schema":"INSPIRE BAI","value":"Peter.van.Loock.1"}],"first_name":"Peter","uuid":"265d261f-02f7-48c2-995c-f57429d5778f","recid":2566049}],"publication_info":[{"journal_volume":"99","pubinfo_freetext":"Phys. Rev. A 99, 062308 (2019)","artid":"062308","material":"publication","year":2019,"journal_title":"Phys.Rev.A"}],"citeable":true,"$schema":"https://inspirehep.net/schemas/records/hep.json","references":[{"reference":{"texkey":"PhysRevLett.81.5932","dois":["10.1103/PhysRevLett.81.5932"],"label":"1","publication_info":{"journal_volume":"81","artid":"5932","year":1998,"page_start":"5932","journal_title":"Phys.Rev.Lett."},"misc":["W. Dür, J. I. Cirac, and P. Zoller"],"authors":[{"full_name":"Briegel, H.-J."}]},"raw_refs":[{"schema":"text","source":"arXiv","value":"[1] H.-J. Briegel, W. Dür, J. I. Cirac, and P. Zoller, Phys. Rev. Lett. 81, 5932 (1998)."}],"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/2730428"}},{"reference":{"texkey":"repeatergenerations","dois":["10.1038/srep20463"],"label":"2","publication_info":{"journal_volume":"6","artid":"20463","year":2016,"journal_title":"Sci.Rep."},"misc":["N. Lütkenhaus, M. D. Lukin, and L. Jiang","EP , article"],"authors":[{"full_name":"Muralidharan, S."},{"full_name":"Li, L."},{"full_name":"Kim, J."}]},"raw_refs":[{"schema":"text","source":"arXiv","value":"[2] S. Muralidharan, L. Li, J. Kim, N. Lütkenhaus, M. D. Lukin, and L. Jiang, Scientific Reports 6, 20463 EP (2016), article."}],"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/2731699"}},{"reference":{"texkey":"PhysRevLett.112.250501","urls":[{"value":"http://dx.doi.org/ 10.1103/PhysRevLett.112.250501"}],"label":"3","publication_info":{"journal_volume":"112","artid":"250501","year":2014,"journal_title":"Phys.Rev.Lett."},"misc":["N. Lütkenhaus, M. D. Lukin, and L. Jiang"]