":"[31] T. M. Stace and S. D. Barrett, Phys. Rev. A 81, 022317 (2010)."}],"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/2950144"}},{"reference":{"texkey":"Note3","urls":[{"value":"https://github.com/"},{"value":"https://github.com/schmidtfrk/CSSlogBM"}],"label":"32","misc":["The script can be found at","schmidtfrk/CSSlogBM"]},"raw_refs":[{"schema":"text","source":"arXiv","value":"[32] The script can be found at https://github.com/ schmidtfrk/CSSlogBM."}]},{"reference":{"texkey":"Note4","label":"33","misc":["Note that the logical BM efficiency of general QPC(n, m) is known when considering loss and standard ZZ=1 BMs [6, 7]. However, here we also consider combinations of different guaranteed-information BMs, breaking the block structure of the gained information and thus rendering the technique of Refs. [6, 7] inapplicable"]},"raw_refs":[{"schema":"text","source":"arXiv","value":"[33] Note that the logical BM efficiency of general QPC(n, m) is known when considering loss and standard ZZ=1 BMs [6, 7]. However, here we also consider combinations of different guaranteed-information BMs, breaking the block structure of the gained information and thus rendering the technique of Refs. [6, 7] inapplicable."}]},{"reference":{"texkey":"Note5","label":"34","misc":["As the bounds for the linear-optics BMs derived in Refs. [5, 9] exclude feedforward, it is still open whether a logical BM efficiency greater than 1 - 2-(n+m-1) and possibly approaching 1 - 2-nm can be achieved with ancilla-free static linear optics and general QPC(n, m). For the particular case of QPC(2,2), however, our optimal guaranteed-information formation combined with our reduction of arbitrary transversal linear-optics BMs to the set of guaranteed-information BMs (see the discussion after Theorem 4) implies that 1-2-3 = 7 8 is already the ultimate limit for ancilla-free static transversal linear optics.)"]},"raw_refs":[{"schema":"text","source":"arXiv","value":"[34] As the bounds for the linear-optics BMs derived in Refs. [5, 9] exclude feedforward, it is still open whether a logical BM efficiency greater than 1 − 2−(n+m−1) and possibly approaching 1 − 2−nm can be achieved with ancilla-free static linear optics and general QPC(n, m). For the particular case of QPC(2,2), however, our optimal guaranteed-information formation combined with our reduction of arbitrary transversal linear-optics BMs to the set of guaranteed-information BMs (see the discussion after Theorem 4) implies that 1−2−3 = 7 8 is already the ultimate limit for ancilla-free static transversal linear optics.)."}]},{"reference":{"texkey":"Note6","label":"35","misc":["See previous footnote"]},"raw_refs":[{"schema":"text","source":"arXiv","value":"[35] See previous footnote."}]},{"reference":{"texkey":"Note7","label":"36","misc":["Notice that a code is just defined as a subspace and relabeling codewords does not matter at all. The Shor code in Ref. [13] is equal to our defined QPC(3,3) up to a logical Hadamard gate"]},"raw_refs":[{"schema":"text","source":"arXiv","value":"[36] Notice that a code is just defined as a subspace and relabeling codewords does not matter at all. The Shor code in Ref. [13] is equal to our defined QPC(3,3) up to a logical Hadamard gate."}]},{"reference":{"texkey":"Note8","label":"37","misc":["Note that the product of a logical operator and a stabilizer is also a logical operator"]},"raw_refs":[{"schema":"text","source":"arXiv","value":"[37] Note that the product of a logical operator and a stabilizer is also a logical operator."}]},{"reference":{"texkey":"topocodelecture","label":"38","publication_info":{"year":2014},"title":{"title":"Lectures on topological codes and quantum computation"},"authors":[{"full_name":"Browne, D."}]},"raw_refs":[{"schema":"text","source":"arXiv","value":"[38] D. Browne, “Lectures on topological codes and quantum computation,” (2014)."}]},{"reference":{"texkey":"lidar2013quantum","label":"39","publication_info":{"year":2013},"misc":["Quantum error correction, 1st ed. (Cambridge,)"],"authors":[{"full_name":"Lidar, D.A."}]},"raw_refs":[{"schema":"text","source":"arXiv","value":"[39] D. A. Lidar, ed., Quantum error correction, 1st ed. (Cambridge, 2013)."}]},{"reference":{"texkey":"1410.0069","label":"40","publication_info":{"journal_volume":"91","artid":"032330","year":2015,"journal_title":"Phys.Rev.A"},"authors":[{"full_name":"Kubica, A."},{"full_name":"Beverland, M.E."}]},"raw_refs":[{"schema":"text","source":"arXiv","value":"[40] A. Kubica and M. E. Beverland, Phys. Rev. A 91, 032330 (2015)."}],"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/2707393"}},{"reference":{"texkey":"eppingcomparison","label":"41","publication_info":{"journal_volume":"18","artid":"053036","year":2016,"journal_title":"New J.Phys."},"misc":["N"],"authors":[{"full_name":"Epping, M."},{"full_name":"Kampermann, H."},{"full_name":"Bru, D."}]},"raw_refs":[{"schema":"text","source":"arXiv","value":"[41] M. Epping, H. Kampermann, and D. Bruß, New Journal of Physics 18, 053036 (2016)."}],"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/2795122"}},{"reference":{"texkey":"Note9","label":"42","misc":["We can also exchange ZZ and XX measurements and the argument remains the same if we look at the dual lattice"]},"raw_refs":[{"schema":"text","source":"arXiv","value":"[42] We can also exchange ZZ and XX measurements and the argument remains the same if we look at the dual lattice."}]},{"reference":{"texkey":"plobhistory","urls":[{"value":"http://arxiv.org/abs/arXiv:1708.07142"}],"arxiv_eprint":"1708.07142","label":"43","publication_info":{"year":2017},"title":{"title":"Routing entanglement in the quantum internet"},"authors":[{"full_name":"Pant, M."},{"full_name":"Krovi, H."},{"full_name":"Towsley, D."},{"full_name":"Tassiulas, L."},{"full_name":"Jiang, L."},{"full_name":"Basu, P."},{"full_name":"Englund, D."},{"full_name":"Guha, S."}]},"raw_refs":[{"schema":"text","source":"arXiv","value":"[43] M. Pant, H. Krovi, D. Towsley, L. Tassiulas, L. Jiang, P. Basu, D. Englund, and S. Guha, “Routing entanglement in the quantum internet,” (2017), arXiv:1708.07142."}],"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/2699147"}}],"number_of_pages":21,"preprint_date":"2018-12-21","author_count":2,"public_notes":[{"source":"arXiv","value":"21 pages, 14 figures"}],"earliest_date":"2018-12-21","facet_author_name":["989835_Frank Schmidt","2566049_Peter van Loock"],"core":true,"license":[{"license":"arXiv nonexclusive-distrib 1.0","material":"preprint","url":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/"}],"_oai":{"sets":["Literature"],"id":"oai:inspirehep.net:3047907","updated":"2026-02-09T15:27:44.898434"},"journal_title_variants":["Phys. Rev. A","Phys.Rev.A"],"arxiv_eprints":[{"categories":["quant-ph"],"value":"1812.09278"}],"referenced_authors_bais":["H.J.Bernstein.1","K.A.Suominen.1","L.M.Pant.1","D.Towsley.1","T.C.Ralph.1","Peter.van.Loock.1","M.D.Lukin.2","S.W.Lee.2","N.Delfosse.1","N.Lutkenhaus.1","Ady.Mann.1","G.Zemor.1","A.Olivo.2","A.Kubica.1","J.I.Cirac.1","R.Laflamme.1","P.Zoller.1","Peter.Van.1","L.Banchi.1","K.Azuma.1","K.Banaszek.1","A.Gilchrist.1","M.M.Wilde.6","T.M.Stace.1","W.Wasilewski.1","S.Muralidharan.2","S.L.Braunstein.1","P.Bertani.1","M.E.Beverland.1","M.Takeoka.1","Jae.Jun.Kim.1","M.Epping.1","S.Guha.2","J.Calsamiglia.1","C.Ottaviani.1","H.Krovi.1","W.P.Grice.1","H.Weinfurter.1","J.L.Jiang.1","K.Mattle.1","R.Laurenza.1","L.Tassiulas.1","E.Knill.1","M.Reck.1","S.Pirandola.2","Dagmar.Bruss.1","H.K.Lo.2","K.Park.7","G.J.Milburn.1","P.Basu.3","H.Jeong.4","S.D.Barrett.1","D.Englund.1","F.Grosshans.1","F.Ewert.1","H.Kampermann.1","W.Dur.2","K.Tamaki.2","M.Bergmann.1","H.J.Briegel.2","D.Gottesman.1","L.J.Li.2","A.J.F.Hayes.1","M.Michler.1","A.Zeilinger.1"],"figures":[{"filename":"repeater_scheme.png","material":"preprint","caption":"A channel of distance $L$ is split into multiple segments of length $L_0=\\frac{L}{\\mathcal{N}}$ (here $\\mathcal{N}=3$). The first station encodes the quantum information within an error correcting code and sends it through 2$N$ parallel bosonic-mode quantum channels (solid arrow) to the next station. A logical Bell state is already prepared in this station and a logical BM is performed on the incoming quantum code and on one half of the logical Bell state in order to teleport the quantum information into the other half of the prepared logical Bell state. This code is then again send to the next station (black solid arrow), while only $2\\cdot k$ noiseless classical bits (dashed arrow) are transmitted in order to keep track of the required Pauli corrections due to the teleportation. This procedure is repeated until the last repeater station where an overall Pauli correction is applied and the code gets decoded or is otherwise consumed in some application.","label":"fig:qr_scheme","source":"arxiv","key":"b7800f402dbe85be4c0aedc2e55a51eb","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/b7800f402dbe85be4c0aedc2e55a51eb"},{"filename":"qpc22figure.png","material":"preprint","caption":" : ","label":"fig:qpc22","source":"arxiv","key":"ef250bd53c87d57952d28752a662c6b9","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/ef250bd53c87d57952d28752a662c6b9"},{"filename":"qpc22figure_with_loss.png","material":"preprint","caption":" : ","label":"fig:qpc22","source":"arxiv","key":"9329332845e3052ecda16fc283f6ba0a","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/9329332845e3052ecda16fc283f6ba0a"},{"filename":"qpc22figure_lin.png","material":"preprint","caption":" : Logical BM on QPC(2,2). The blue circles denote qubits of the first copy of QPC(2,2), while the red circles denote qubits of the second copy of QPC(2,2). Physical BMs are denoted by dashed ellipses and are performed on corresponding qubit pairs. The two blocks of each code are indicated by the black boxes. (a) Unconstrained loss-free case. Since all qubit pairs are intact, a logical BM always succeeds using unconstrained physical BMs. (b) Unconstraint lossy case.  Photon 3 of the second code is lost, such that we cannot perform a BM on the third qubit pair. However, qubit pairs 1 and 2 give us information about $\\overline{XX}$ and e.g. qubit pairs 1 and 4 still give us information about $\\overline{ZZ}$. As a consequence we are still able to perform the logical BM. In (c) and (d) we consider the linear-optics constraint. This means for the physical BMs we always obtain the $ZZ$ measurement result, but we only obtain the XX measurement result if the $ZZ$ measurement yielded -1. We show the possible $ZZ$ measurement results and mark those results where a logical BM is possible in green. (c) No photons are lost and only one of the four possibilities is a failure. (d) Loss of photon 3 in code 2 and now two of the four possibilities are failures. : Caption not extracted","label":"fig:qpc22","source":"arxiv","key":"8dbb3c11ba3b50a68c729f90d02d30b3","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/8dbb3c11ba3b50a68c729f90d02d30b3"},{"filename":"qpc22figure_with_loss_lin.png","material":"preprint","caption":" : Caption not extracted","label":"fig:qpc22","source":"arxiv","key":"e93963fa25f147bb36e4e0e29db81f61","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/e93963fa25f147bb36e4e0e29db81f61"},{"filename":"toric22_1.png","material":"preprint","caption":" : ","label":"fig:formations","source":"arxiv","key":"90c868afb6a0991f98c9a21aaf60ef06","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/90c868afb6a0991f98c9a21aaf60ef06"},{"filename":"surface22_xyz.png","material":"preprint","caption":" : ","label":"fig:formations","source":"arxiv","key":"b0f6965e656b87752564c987048da492","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/b0f6965e656b87752564c987048da492"},{"filename":"colorcode_arrangement2.png","material":"preprint","caption":" :  : (a) and (b) show good measurement formations for the surface(2,2) code resulting in a no-loss efficiency of $1-2^{-3}$ and $1-2^{-5}$. Black bars denote $XX$=1 measurements, red bars denote $ZZ$=1 measurements, and yellow bars denote $YY$=1 measurements. (c) and (d) show good measurement formations for the Steane code resulting in a no-loss efficiency of $1-2^{-3}$ and $1-2^{-5}$. (e) shows the measurement formation which gives a no-loss efficiency of $1-2^{-(n+m-1)}$ for QPC($n,m$).","label":"fig:formations","source":"arxiv","key":"d0d8182e5201d2a62e75226ce32b6495","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/d0d8182e5201d2a62e75226ce32b6495"},{"filename":"colorcode_arrangement3_optimal.png","material":"preprint","caption":" : Caption not extracted","label":"fig:formations","source":"arxiv","key":"4a85432c03ad34f3b40d5d811ba8ae73","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/4a85432c03ad34f3b40d5d811ba8ae73"},{"filename":"qpc_arrangement.png","material":"preprint","caption":" : Caption not extracted","label":"fig:formations","source":"arxiv","key":"613b1a8a89009991854660454251c670","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/613b1a8a89009991854660454251c670"},{"filename":"qpc22loss_boxed.png","material":"preprint","caption":"Efficiency of the logical BM for QPC(2,2) in the presence of photon loss. The orange line represents the use of our new static linear-optics logical BM employing a combination of $XX=1$, $YY=1$, and $ZZ=1$ BMs, while the blue line corresponds to the already known logical BM efficiency where only $ZZ=1$ BMs are used with static linear optics \\cite{Ewertqpcprl}.","label":"fig:qpc22loss","source":"arxiv","key":"e2147f74c7d3883e4bd28da06b70d799","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/e2147f74c7d3883e4bd28da06b70d799"},{"filename":"comparision_small_codes_noadvanced_boxed.png","material":"preprint","caption":" : ","label":"fig:comparisonadvanced","source":"arxiv","key":"146b95ba7b457cf3ab5f923b7565d3a5","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/146b95ba7b457cf3ab5f923b7565d3a5"},{"filename":"comparision_small_codes_50advanced_boxed.png","material":"preprint","caption":" :  : Efficiency of the logical BM in the presence of photon loss. Solid lines denote the use of only $ZZ$=1 measurements and dashed lines denote the use of $XX$,$YY$ and $ZZ$=1 measurements. The green line $\\eta_\\text{log}=\\eta$ corresponds to direct, unencoded DR-qubit transmission without a quantum repeater and is therefore the benchmark that should be outperformed. Red corresponds to the Steane code, blue corresponds to QPC(4,2) and black corresponds to surface(3,2). (a) $p_{\\text{adv}}=0$, (b) $p_{\\text{adv}}=0.5$, (c)$p_{\\text{adv}}=0.75$.","label":"fig:comparisonadvanced","source":"arxiv","key":"42452ea58dd2eb397ba4840b04aa5151","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/42452ea58dd2eb397ba4840b04aa5151"},{"filename":"comparision_small_codes_75advanced_boxed.png","material":"preprint","caption":" : Caption not extracted","label":"fig:comparisonadvanced","source":"arxiv","key":"e26438d9f9b91acb083b3f6aed7e4820","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/e26438d9f9b91acb083b3f6aed7e4820"},{"filename":"planar_boundaries.png","material":"preprint","caption":"Lattice on a planar surface with two smooth and two rough boundaries. Smooth boundaries are rough in the dual lattice and rough boundaries are smooth in the dual lattice.","label":"fig:planarboundaries","source":"arxiv","key":"584b0c71b63d8905a94272b9ab6f76fd","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/584b0c71b63d8905a94272b9ab6f76fd"},{"filename":"surface32.png","material":"preprint","caption":"Numbering of the surface(3,2) code.","label":"fig:surfacenumbering","source":"arxiv","key":"a799371460e4520b1c7c8c02c08e459d","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/a799371460e4520b1c7c8c02c08e459d"},{"filename":"colorcodes488.png","material":"preprint","caption":"Family of 4-8-8 tessellated triangular color codes. E.g. a code with a code distance of 5 can be obtained by only considering the triangle above the dotted line labeled by ’d=5’.","label":"fig:colorcode488","source":"arxiv","key":"d9acb505465000d855c5356fa26d4b6d","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/d9acb505465000d855c5356fa26d4b6d"},{"filename":"steane_code_numbering.png","material":"preprint","caption":"The Steane code as a planar color code. The qubit labeling was chosen in this particular way in order to be consistent with the definition of the Steane code given in Ref. \\cite{Nielsen:2011:QCQ:1972505}.","label":"fig:colorcode_steane","source":"arxiv","key":"2eba0d368dd5ee771382c2c308cb0391","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/2eba0d368dd5ee771382c2c308cb0391"},{"filename":"comparisonnonlinear_boxed_2.png","material":"preprint","caption":"Performance of the Steane code (red), surface(3,2) (black), 23-qubit Golay code (yellow), and QPC(4,2) (blue) with unconstrained BMs. Green denotes direct transmission without a code. We also showed the Golay code for a comparison with larger codes.","label":"fig:comparisonnonlinear","source":"arxiv","key":"7722d1d23f0cfc0c3200642e6b0090a7","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/7722d1d23f0cfc0c3200642e6b0090a7"},{"filename":"toric_allowed_1.png","material":"preprint","caption":" : ","label":"fig:toric_lin_comb_overall","source":"arxiv","key":"639e6fd1f0c9fd9aedf1af4561699937","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/639e6fd1f0c9fd9aedf1af4561699937"},{"filename":"toric_allowed_2.png","material":"preprint","caption":" : ","label":"fig:toric_lin_comb_overall","source":"arxiv","key":"1cafb7ccaf7aab3c80940f9892618729","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/1cafb7ccaf7aab3c80940f9892618729"},{"filename":"toric_allowed_3.png","material":"preprint","caption":" :  : Several example configurations for the combinatorics of the logical BM efficiency for the planar surface code with linear optics. All empty squares have the value 0. In general, there are ($n$-1) columns and $m$ rows. The examples (a)-(d) are allowed, whereas combination (e) is not allowed, i.e. it corresponds to a failed logical BM.","label":"fig:toric_lin_comb_overall","source":"arxiv","key":"bd0d5cd2c02d9ed97d003ee0695f4c33","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/bd0d5cd2c02d9ed97d003ee0695f4c33"},{"filename":"toric_allowed_4.png","material":"preprint","caption":" : Caption not extracted","label":"fig:toric_lin_comb_overall","source":"arxiv","key":"3bbc4f8fb3b7f30d790ea28c6748588a","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/3bbc4f8fb3b7f30d790ea28c6748588a"},{"filename":"toric_not_allowed.png","material":"preprint","caption":" : Caption not extracted","label":"fig:toric_lin_comb_overall","source":"arxiv","key":"bf3fe475ae68c56a54a2e8dcdc4fe090","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/bf3fe475ae68c56a54a2e8dcdc4fe090"},{"filename":"toric_forbidden_1.png","material":"preprint","caption":" : width=0.45\\linewidth","label":"fig:toric_fail_subtraction","source":"arxiv","key":"e24f4e61ac91d28b81237dce3667f20b","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/e24f4e61ac91d28b81237dce3667f20b"},{"filename":"toric_forbidden_2.png","material":"preprint","caption":" : Two configurations which give rise to the subtraction in \\cref{eq:toric4m}. (a) The interruption takes place in the (j-1)\\textsuperscript{th} row such that a number of $z_{j-2}$ counted combinations is invalid. (b)The interruption takes place in the (j-2)\\textsuperscript{th} row such that a number of $z_{j-3}$ counted combinations is invalid. Taking care of all these cases gives the sum in \\autoref{eq:toric4m}. : Caption not extracted","label":"fig:toric_fail_subtraction","source":"arxiv","key":"a88fee2f4ed689b2410d77d800017df1","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/a88fee2f4ed689b2410d77d800017df1"},{"filename":"visual_surface_mix_1.png","material":"preprint","caption":" : ","label":"fig:visualization_planar_surface_linear_optic_mix","source":"arxiv","key":"00014932ffe0ac69ffa9114841b90c7d","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/00014932ffe0ac69ffa9114841b90c7d"},{"filename":"visual_surface_mix_2.png","material":"preprint","caption":" :  : Visualization of codewords that correspond to $X$-stabilizer generators within a surface(5,5) code. The dotted red line is a visual aid and shows the diagonal where we perform $ZZ$=1 measurements. In (a) we can see the support of an $X$-stabilizer generator (marked as blue lines) whose support contains edges of the diagonal. In (b) we can see the support of the operator after multiplying two adjacent $X$ stabilizer generators, resulting in a movement of the edges which lie on the diagonal and belong to the support of the operator. (c) shows that the procedure performed in (b) can only be applied a finite number of times due to the boundary of the plane.","label":"fig:visualization_planar_surface_linear_optic_mix","source":"arxiv","key":"714e279f9627bfa95cd5fe22ae076820","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/714e279f9627bfa95cd5fe22ae076820"},{"filename":"visual_surface_mix_3.png","material":"preprint","caption":" : Caption not extracted","label":"fig:visualization_planar_surface_linear_optic_mix","source":"arxiv","key":"1912d82fb7814b5d2c0b54daf600ce62","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/1912d82fb7814b5d2c0b54daf600ce62"},{"filename":"surface_mix_n_neq_m.png","material":"preprint","caption":"One example of the formation of physical BMs with different guaranteed-information of a planar surface(n,m)-code, where $n>m>$1. Red edges correspond to guaranteed-$ZZ$=1 measurements and black edges correspond to guaranteed-$XX$=1 measurements, such that $\\overline{ZZ}$-information is always given without loss. For the $\\overline{XX}$ information only one of the red edges BMs need to give additional information. If $m>n>1$ one goes to the dual-lattice picture and gets the same lattice as in the Figure, but then red edges correspond to guaranteed-$XX$ BMs and black edges to guaranteed-$ZZ$ BMs. The diagonal path of red edges in the case $n=m$ is a special case of the zig-zag.","label":"fig:toric_mix_34","source":"arxiv","key":"b21278d721c5fdd3166b87e6e7943dc7","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/b21278d721c5fdd3166b87e6e7943dc7"}],"inspire_categories":[{"term":"Quantum Physics","source":"arxiv"}],"first_author":{"full_name":"Schmidt, Frank","last_name":"Schmidt","first_name":"Frank","recid":989835},"control_number":3047907,"dois":[{"material":"publication","source":"arXiv","value":"10.1103/PhysRevA.99.062308"}],"document_type":["article"],"texkeys":["Schmidt:2018wpw"],"abstracts":[{"source":"arXiv","value":"We show how the efficiency of a logical Bell measurement (BM) can be calculated for arbitrary CSS codes with the experimentally important constraint of using only transversal static linear-optical BMs on the physical single-photon qubit level. For this purpose, we utilize the codes' description in terms of stabilizers in order to calculate general efficiencies for the loss-free case, but also for specific cases including photon loss. These efficiencies can be, for instance, used for obtaining transmission rates of all-optical quantum repeaters. In the loss-free case, we demonstrate that the important class of CSS codes with identical physical-qubit support for the two logical Pauli ($Z$ and $X$) operators can only achieve a logical BM efficiency of $\\frac{1}{2}$ if one always employs the same ancilla-free static linear optical BMs on the physical level. We apply our methods to various CSS codes including two-dimensional planar color and planar surface codes. We then find that in many cases, the fixed use of the standard linear optical BM for all physical BMs is suboptimal and performing linear optical transformations before doing the standard linear optical BM (still without any ancillary photons and without any feedforward) can increase the efficiency enormously. In fact, using this generalization in the no-loss (or sometimes also in the low-loss) case allows us, on the one hand, to improve the logical BM efficiency of quantum parity codes compared to previously known results and, on the other hand, it also enables us to enhance the efficiency of two-dimensional planar color codes, whose efficiency is otherwise subject to the above $\\frac{1}{2}$ bound, to arbitrarily close to unity.","abstract_source_suggest":{"input":"arXiv"}}],"primary_arxiv_category":["quant-ph"],"titles":[{"source":"arXiv","title":"Efficiencies of logical Bell measurements on CSS codes with static linear optics"}],"curated":false},"created":"2025-10-03T13:02:12.816859+00:00","id":"3047907","updated":"2026-02-09T15:27:44.898434+00:00","links":{"bibtex":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/3047907?format=bibtex","latex-eu":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/3047907?format=latex-eu","latex-us":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/3047907?format=latex-us","json":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/3047907?format=json","json-expanded":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/3047907?format=json-expanded","cv":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/3047907?format=cv","citations":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/?q=refersto%3Arecid%3A3047907"}},{"metadata":{"citation_count":39,"citation_count_without_self_citations":30,"authors":[{"raw_affiliations":[{"value":"Centre for Quantum Technologies, National University of Singapore, 117543 Singapore, Singapore"},{"value":"Department of Physics, National University of Singapore, 117543 Singapore, Singapore"}],"full_name_unicode_normalized":"jia, zhian","affiliations":[{"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/institutions/910227"},"value":"Singapore Natl. U. (main)"},{"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/institutions/904194"},"value":"Singapore Natl. U."}],"last_name":"Jia","uuid":"f0ffd161-c7f0-4907-95a0-a79c3be1dcbc","affiliations_identifiers":[{"schema":"GRID","value":"grid.4280.e"},{"schema":"ROR","value":"https://ror.org/01tgyzw49"},{"schema":"ROR","value":"https://ror.org/01tgyzw49"}],"full_name":"Jia, Zhian","curated_relation":true,"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/authors/1810314"},"ids":[{"schema":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-8588-173X"},{"schema":"INSPIRE BAI","value":"Zhian.Jia.1"}],"first_name":"Zhian","recid":1810314}],"publication_info":[{"journal_volume":"09","artid":"147","year":2024,"journal_record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/journals/1213103"},"page_start":"147","journal_title":"JHEP"}],"documents":[{"filename":"document","fulltext":true,"source":"Springer","key":"e842d