osing":"Springer","url":"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/"},{"license":"CC BY 4.0","material":"preprint","url":"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/"}],"_oai":{"sets":["Literature"],"id":"oai:inspirehep.net:2787056","updated":"2026-02-09T13:57:21.502154"},"journal_title_variants":["JHEP"],"arxiv_eprints":[{"categories":["quant-ph","cond-mat.str-el","hep-th","math-ph","math.MP","math.QA"],"value":"2405.09277"}],"referenced_authors_bais":["A.Osumanu.1","J.Preskill.1","F.A.Bais.1","Ludovic.Fraser.Taliente.1","C.M.Chang.1","Zeng.Bing.Chen.1","C.C.Zhang.1","Victor.V.Albert.1","Sungwoo.Hong.1","O.Guehne.1","S.Sarkar.173","N.Seiberg.1","J.I.Cirac.1","Xi.Yin.1","Luigi.Amico.1","R.Orus.1","Chiara.Macchiavello.1","D.S.W.Gould.1","Shu.Heng.Shao.1","J.Fuchs.1","P.R.S.Gomes.1","J.Eisert.1","Y.R.Bao.1","R.B.Griffiths.1","S.Z.Li.1","Hannah.Tillim.1","J.Froehlich.1","V.Gheorghiu.1","F.Verstraete.1","K.Inamura.1","R.Qu.3","L.Bhardwaj.1","M.Aguado.1","Y.Z.Zhen.1","T.T.Dumitrescu.1","Lea.E.Bottini.1","Ying.Hsuan.Lin.1","Q.R.Wang.1","S.Y.Looi.1","C.Teleman.1","A.Y.Kitaev.1","C.Schweigert.1","Kantaro.Ohmori.1","Liang.Kong.1","Konstantinos.Roumpedakis.1","B.Fortescue.1","D.Kaszlikowski.1","B.Zeng.1","T.D.Brennan.1","J.GarreRubio.1","Christopher.Fechisin.1","D.PerezGarcia.3","L.Chang.3","Kenneth.A.Intriligator.1","Anton.N.Kapustin.1","W.Dur.2","N.Schuch.2","A.Platschorre.1","H.J.Briegel.2","B.C.Sanders.1","Sheng.Tan.1","C.G.Brell.1","M.Walschaers.1","D.Gaiotto.1","Xiao.Gang.Wen.1","V.Parigi.1","M.R.Gaberdiel.1","J.McGreevy.1","A.R.de.Alarcon.1","O.Buerschaper.2","Apoorv.Tiwari.1","B.Yan.9","Y.Tachikawa.1","Sheng.Jie.Huang.1","F.E.S.Steinhoff.1","F.Bonetti.1","Clay.Cordova.1","J.Kulp.1","C.Ritz.1","J.Wang.128","B.J.Schroers.1","D.Pajer.1","A.Keet.1","Laurens.Lootens.1","Kai.Feng.Chen.1","J.Alicea.1","S.S.Hosseini.2","Melisa.Rossi.1","Robert.Raussendorf.1","Nathanan.Tantivasadakarn.1","M.Van.den.Nest.2","J.K.Slingerland.3","N.Miklin.1","L.Gladden.1","M.A.Nielsen.2","M.T.Cheng.1","P.K.Osei.1","J.C.Bridgeman.1","Andreas.W.W.Ludwig.1","I.Garcia.Etxebarria.1","F.L.Xiong.1","J.M.Mombelli.1","W.Ji.4","M.Freedman.1","W.Xu.28","Yi.Nan.Wang.1","D.E.Browne.1","Clement.Delcamp.1","D.S.Freed.1","S.X.Cui.1","D.Markham.1","M.Troyer.1","Dagmar.Bruss.1","Zheng.Han.Wang.1","F.Girelli.1","P.Chen.19","A.Feiguin.1","L.A.Molnar.1","J.H.Zheng.1","S.Trebst.1","Zhian.Jia.1","Z.H.Zhang.13","Fabio.Apruzzi.1","C.W.Son.1","L.Yu.3","R.Thorngren.1","M.Hein.5","S.Schafer.Nameki.1","D.W.Moore.1","D.Aasen.1","Gregory.W.Moore.1","Yi.Fan.Wang.1","I.Runkel.1","R.Luo.1","N.Yu.7","Sahand.Seifnashri.1","M.Christandl.1","Vlatko.Vedral.1","N.Treps.2","M.G.Huber.2","W.F.Cao.3","Z.Komargodski.1","T.Lan.1","B.Willett.1"],"figures":[{"filename":"ClusterGraph.png","material":"preprint","caption":"An illustration of cluster graph. The red vertices represent odd vertices, while the teal vertices represent even vertices. The vertices are labeled with the ordering from $1$ to $20$, and examples of local ordering for edges connected to odd vertex $v_7$ are shown as \\textcircled{1}, $\\cdots$, and \\textcircled{4}.","label":"fig:cluster-graph","source":"arxiv","key":"0d71e0109d61037f03e142fa16af99a8","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/0d71e0109d61037f03e142fa16af99a8"},{"filename":"FigClusterLattice.png","material":"preprint","caption":"An example of cluster lattice on a torus $\\mathbb{T}^2$. We first create a cellulation of $\\mathbb{T}^2$ and regard the vertices as odd vertices (depicted as red vertices in the figure). An orientation is assigned to each edge. Then, for each edge, we add an even vertex (represented as teal vertices in the figure). These even vertices are all bivalent. The original edge now becomes two edges connected to the even vertex, one input edge, and one output edge.","label":"fig:cluster-lattice-graph","source":"arxiv","key":"abf6ee68c5da46d4952feaffe8d34e37","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/abf6ee68c5da46d4952feaffe8d34e37"},{"filename":"FigHopfTN2d.png","material":"preprint","caption":"Illustration of the Hopf tensor network representation of the Hopf cluster state. On the left, there is a cluster graph with a globally ordered edge set, $e_1, \\cdots, e_{12}$. For each vertex, the neighboring edges have a local ordering induced by global ordering of edge set. For an odd vertex, this local ordering determines how we place the comultiplication component on each edge and the edge direction determine whether we take antipode; for an even vertex, the local ordering determines the order for taking multiplication. On the right, we present the Hopf tensor network for the corresponding Hopf cluster state, where $\\lambda$ represents Haar integral and $\\mu$ represnets the multiplication map.","label":"fig:cluster-TN2d","source":"arxiv","key":"3a30f60167e0edbb5f7db7227838c59f","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/3a30f60167e0edbb5f7db7227838c59f"},{"filename":"FigTensorNet.png","material":"preprint","caption":"The Hopf tensor network for an one dimensional Hopf cluster state.","label":"fig:cluster-TN","source":"arxiv","key":"222bdd381f37404dfe98328750fb8b5c","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/222bdd381f37404dfe98328750fb8b5c"},{"filename":"Fig_QDCluster.png","material":"preprint","caption":"Illustration depicting the transformation from local stabilizers for the Hopf cluster state into the face and vertex operators of the Hopf quantum double model. By folding the 1d cluster lattice into a zigzag configuration, a 2d lattice is formed, represented by gray dotted lines in the figure, with vertices depicted as stars.","label":"fig:QDcluster","source":"arxiv","key":"0481b883355b640195ad14ce782c9c3b","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/0481b883355b640195ad14ce782c9c3b"},{"filename":"Fig_ToricCode.png","material":"preprint","caption":"Illustration depicting the quasi-1d toric code model represented in a chess-board layout: (a) periodic boundary conditions and (b) open boundary conditions. The vertex plaquettes are depicted as light green triangles, while the face plaquettes are represented as light orange triangles. The red circles represent odd vertices, while the teal squares denote even vertices in the original Hopf cluster lattice.","label":"fig:QDRibbon","source":"arxiv","key":"8b08fe17f589060d8bb408c66a81a3aa","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/8b08fe17f589060d8bb408c66a81a3aa"},{"filename":"FigLadder.png","material":"preprint","caption":"Depiction of the ladder lattice of SymTFT sandwich for Hopf lattice gauge theory.","label":"fig:ladder","source":"arxiv","key":"8733627a84907e74b4637af152e3e7c9","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/8733627a84907e74b4637af152e3e7c9"}],"inspire_categories":[{"term":"Quantum Physics","source":"arxiv"},{"term":"Condensed Matter","source":"arxiv"},{"term":"Theory-HEP","source":"arxiv"},{"term":"Math and Math Physics","source":"arxiv"}],"first_author":{"emails":["giannjia@foxmail.com"],"affiliations_identifiers":[{"schema":"GRID","value":"grid.4280.e"},{"schema":"ROR","value":"https://ror.org/01tgyzw49"},{"schema":"ROR","value":"https://ror.org/01tgyzw49"}],"full_name":"Jia, Zhian","ids":[{"schema":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-8588-173X"},{"schema":"INSPIRE BAI","value":"Zhian.Jia.1"}],"last_name":"Jia","first_name":"Zhian","recid":1810314},"control_number":2787056,"dois":[{"source":"Springer","value":"10.1007/JHEP09(2024)147"},{"material":"publication","source":"arXiv","value":"10.1007/JHEP09(2024)147"}],"document_type":["article"],"texkeys":["Jia:2024bng"],"abstracts":[{"source":"Springer","value":"Cluster states are crucial resources for measurement-based quantum computation (MBQC). It exhibits symmetry-protected topological (SPT) order, thus also playing a crucial role in studying topological phases. We present the construction of cluster states based on Hopf algebras. By generalizing the finite group valued qudit to a Hopf algebra valued qudit and introducing the generalized Pauli-X operator based on the regular action of the Hopf algebra, as well as the generalized Pauli-Z operator based on the irreducible representation action on the Hopf algebra, we develop a comprehensive theory of Hopf qudits. We demonstrate that non-invertible symmetry naturally emerges for Hopf qudits. Subsequently, for a bipartite graph termed the cluster graph, we assign the identity state and trivial representation state to even and odd vertices, respectively. Introducing the edge entangler as controlled regular action, we provide a general construction of Hopf cluster states. To ensure the commutativity of the edge entangler, we propose a method to construct a cluster lattice for any triangulable manifold. We use the 1d cluster state as an example to illustrate our construction. As this serves as a promising candidate for SPT phases, we construct the gapped Hamiltonian for this scenario and provide a detailed discussion of its non-invertible symmetries. We demonstrate that the 1d cluster state model is equivalent to the quasi-1d Hopf quantum double model with one rough boundary and one smooth boundary. We also discuss the generalization of the Hopf cluster state model to the Hopf ladder model through symmetry topological field theory. Furthermore, we introduce the Hopf tensor network representation of Hopf cluster states by integrating the tensor representation of structure constants with the string diagrams of the Hopf algebra, which can be used to solve the Hopf cluster state model.","abstract_source_suggest":{"input":"Springer"}},{"source":"arXiv","value":"Cluster states are crucial resources for measurement-based quantum computation (MBQC). It exhibits symmetry-protected topological (SPT) order, thus also playing a crucial role in studying topological phases. We present the construction of cluster states based on Hopf algebras. By generalizing the finite group valued qudit to a Hopf algebra valued qudit and introducing the generalized Pauli-X operator based on the regular action of the Hopf algebra, as well as the generalized Pauli-Z operator based on the irreducible representation action on the Hopf algebra, we develop a comprehensive theory of Hopf qudits. We demonstrate that non-invertible symmetry naturally emerges for Hopf qudits. Subsequently, for a bipartite graph termed the cluster graph, we assign the identity state and trivial representation state to even and odd vertices, respectively. Introducing the edge entangler as controlled regular action, we provide a general construction of Hopf cluster states. To ensure the commutativity of the edge entangler, we propose a method to construct a cluster lattice for any triangulable manifold. We use the 1d cluster state as an example to illustrate our construction. As this serves as a promising candidate for SPT phases, we construct the gapped Hamiltonian for this scenario and provide a detailed discussion of its non-invertible symmetries. We demonstrate that the 1d cluster state model is equivalent to the quasi-1d Hopf quantum double model with one rough boundary and one smooth boundary. We also discuss the generalization of the Hopf cluster state model to the Hopf ladder model through symmetry topological field theory. Furthermore, we introduce the Hopf tensor network representation of Hopf cluster states by integrating the tensor representation of structure constants with the string diagrams of the Hopf algebra, which can be used to solve the Hopf cluster state model.","abstract_source_suggest":{"input":"arXiv"}}],"primary_arxiv_category":["quant-ph"],"titles":[{"source":"Springer","title":"Generalized cluster states from Hopf algebras: non-invertible symmetry and Hopf tensor network representation"},{"source":"arXiv","title":"Generalized cluster states from Hopf algebras: non-invertible symmetry and Hopf tensor network representation"}],"imprints":[{"date":"2024-09-20"}],"curated":false},"created":"2024-05-16T15:21:21.898260+00:00","id":"2787056","updated":"2026-02-09T13:57:21.502154+00:00","links":{"bibtex":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/2787056?format=bibtex","latex-eu":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/2787056?format=latex-eu","latex-us":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/2787056?format=latex-us","json":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/2787056?format=json","json-expanded":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/2787056?format=json-expanded","cv":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/2787056?format=cv","citations":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/?q=refersto%3Arecid%3A2787056"}}],"total":30},"links":{"self":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/?q=...taking%20the%20rough%20with%20the%20smooth...&size=10&page=1","next":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/?q=...taking%20the%20rough%20with%20the%20smooth...&size=10&page=2","bibtex":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/?q=...taking%20the%20rough%20with%20the%20smooth...&size=10&page=1&format=bibtex","latex-eu":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/?q=...taking%20the%20rough%20with%20the%20smooth...&size=10&page=1&format=latex-eu","latex-us":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/?q=...taking%20the%20rough%20with%20the%20smooth...&size=10&page=1&format=latex-us","json":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/?q=...taking%20the%20rough%20with%20the%20smooth...&size=10&page=1&format=json","json-expanded":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/?q=...taking%20the%20rough%20with%20the%20smooth...&size=10&page=1&format=json-expanded","cv":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/?q=...taking%20the%20rough%20with%20the%20smooth...&size=10&page=1&format=cv"}}